PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The exact calcium level requires urgent blood gas analysis at the hospital; the condition is highly volatile.
Clinical Course: The tetany will resolve rapidly once intravenous calcium is administered in the hospital. Long-term oral calcium and vitamin D supplementation will be required.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

During any future episodes of facial twitching or hand cramping, immediately call 000. Do not attempt to feed the infant during a spasm due to the high risk of choking.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The parathyroid glands in his neck are underdeveloped and are not producing enough hormone to keep his blood calcium levels normal. Low calcium makes the nerves and muscles misfire, causing the cramps and twitches. He needs calcium given directly into his vein at the hospital immediately.
Actionable Steps: Pack his cardiac medications for the hospital transfer. Do not feed him right now. Follow all instructions from the paramedics.

